WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.240
- We have a special first guest
joining us today, everybody.

00:00:03.240 --> 00:00:05.550
She's made almost no public appearances

00:00:05.550 --> 00:00:08.651
since she was diagnosed with
Multiple Sclerosis last year,

00:00:08.651 --> 00:00:10.140
which as many of you know,

00:00:10.140 --> 00:00:13.500
is a condition that causes
you to be immunocompromised.

00:00:13.500 --> 00:00:16.650
We're blessed that she's doing
well enough to call in today.

00:00:16.650 --> 00:00:20.040
She's done a bunch of
amazing movies and shows.

00:00:20.040 --> 00:00:21.328
I've loved her since I was a kid.

00:00:21.328 --> 00:00:24.750
You can see the third season
of her latest show Dead to Me,

00:00:24.750 --> 00:00:26.910
which is hilarious, right now on Netflix.

00:00:26.910 --> 00:00:27.810
All right, everybody

00:00:27.810 --> 00:00:30.607
let's say hello to Christina Applegate.

00:00:30.607 --> 00:00:34.290
(crowd cheering and clapping)

00:00:34.290 --> 00:00:35.343
- Hi, Kelly Clarkson.

00:00:36.330 --> 00:00:38.700
- Oh my gosh, hi Christina Applegate.

00:00:38.700 --> 00:00:40.526
It's so great to see you.

00:00:40.526 --> 00:00:43.080
I love that we're both not there.

00:00:43.080 --> 00:00:44.460
This is the best interview.

00:00:44.460 --> 00:00:45.664
- We're just not there.

00:00:45.664 --> 00:00:46.530
(Kelly laughing)

00:00:46.530 --> 00:00:49.329
We're just not. Everyone
else is there but us.

00:00:49.329 --> 00:00:50.190
(laughing)

00:00:50.190 --> 00:00:51.720
- We got some things going on Kelly.

00:00:51.720 --> 00:00:54.120
We got some things going on.
- This is live.

00:00:54.120 --> 00:00:57.480
So we were supposed to have
an outdoor lunch together

00:00:57.480 --> 00:01:00.114
and couldn't because of high winds.

00:01:00.114 --> 00:01:01.582
- I ate all the food.

00:01:01.582 --> 00:01:02.970
(Kelly laughing)

00:01:02.970 --> 00:01:04.857
- See, we would make very good friends

00:01:04.857 --> 00:01:06.006
- They sent it to my house

00:01:06.006 --> 00:01:07.787
and I'm sorry I ate all the food.

00:01:07.787 --> 00:01:09.930
- That's okay. I get it.

00:01:09.930 --> 00:01:12.180
You're my sister in life.
That's what that is.

00:01:12.180 --> 00:01:14.550
Well, now I'm sick, so
I'm so sad about it.

00:01:14.550 --> 00:01:16.680
I'm so sad we can't be
there in person together.

00:01:16.680 --> 00:01:19.830
But, I wanna start with this,
'cause this is really cool.

00:01:19.830 --> 00:01:22.020
You just got your star in
the Walk of fame recently

00:01:22.020 --> 00:01:24.690
so it was your only public appearance

00:01:24.690 --> 00:01:26.550
since you were diagnosed with MS. Right?

00:01:26.550 --> 00:01:27.540
How are you feeling?

00:01:27.540 --> 00:01:28.920
- Yeah. Oh boy.

00:01:28.920 --> 00:01:30.678
Look at them feet.

00:01:30.678 --> 00:01:33.840
Yeah, that's the first time, well,

00:01:33.840 --> 00:01:35.880
since filming which was not public,

00:01:35.880 --> 00:01:37.950
but public now obviously for Dead To Me.

00:01:37.950 --> 00:01:39.540
But yeah, it was the first time

00:01:39.540 --> 00:01:42.510
I had to be somewhere with my disability

00:01:42.510 --> 00:01:47.250
and it was interesting
and it was beautiful.

00:01:47.250 --> 00:01:50.130
It was an amazing day and
I feel incredibly blessed

00:01:50.130 --> 00:01:52.920
but at the same time, yeah, it was weird.

00:01:52.920 --> 00:01:54.330
- Yeah. Aw man.

00:01:54.330 --> 00:01:57.330
I hate that anything special like that

00:01:57.330 --> 00:01:58.230
was even tainted like that.

00:01:58.230 --> 00:02:00.900
But it was a cool thing though
for everybody just to get-

00:02:00.900 --> 00:02:02.520
Like I've been a fan of
you since I was a kid.

00:02:02.520 --> 00:02:04.470
I thought it was so cool
your name was Kelly Bundy.

00:02:04.470 --> 00:02:05.793
My name was Kelly.

00:02:05.793 --> 00:02:06.749
(laughing)

00:02:06.749 --> 00:02:07.582
- Yeah, I know.

00:02:07.582 --> 00:02:08.430
- So we've been a fan of you forever

00:02:08.430 --> 00:02:11.010
and so it was cool for us,

00:02:11.010 --> 00:02:14.130
to get to see you honored like that.

00:02:14.130 --> 00:02:17.310
But are you someone who's
naturally optimistic

00:02:17.310 --> 00:02:20.640
when you're sick or when
you're fighting something?

00:02:20.640 --> 00:02:23.775
'Cause I go to humor and very dark humor.

00:02:23.775 --> 00:02:26.523
I don't know how you deal with things.

00:02:28.620 --> 00:02:32.186
- Yeah. My humor shield keeps me okay.

00:02:32.186 --> 00:02:35.550
But of course, down on the
insides you feel the things

00:02:35.550 --> 00:02:37.950
and I do it to kind of deflect

00:02:37.950 --> 00:02:42.450
and then also make people not
be scared to be around me.

00:02:42.450 --> 00:02:43.283
You know?

00:02:43.283 --> 00:02:47.010
When people see me now
as a a disabled person,

00:02:47.010 --> 00:02:50.460
I want them to feel comfortable
that we can laugh about it.

00:02:50.460 --> 00:02:52.540
I have a song that I wrote

00:02:53.375 --> 00:02:55.064
♪ Called to say hey baby ♪

00:02:55.064 --> 00:02:57.434
♪ Hurry down the chimney tonight. ♪

00:02:57.434 --> 00:03:00.392
♪ I can't cause my wheelchair
won't fit down it. ♪

00:03:00.392 --> 00:03:02.670
(crowd laughing)

00:03:02.670 --> 00:03:03.870
- Oh my God.

00:03:03.870 --> 00:03:06.660
See, you're like me.
You'd take humor with it.

00:03:06.660 --> 00:03:11.660
- Yeah. To be honest, being
diagnosed with MS last year

00:03:13.110 --> 00:03:14.760
and what had happened to my body

00:03:14.760 --> 00:03:18.030
to my mind, to my
spirit, to my everything.

00:03:18.030 --> 00:03:21.870
Of course I didn't wanna be
around anyone or talk about it,

00:03:21.870 --> 00:03:23.010
but I had to go to work.

00:03:23.010 --> 00:03:25.830
And I was not forced to go to work,

00:03:25.830 --> 00:03:29.280
but I made sure that we finished the show.

00:03:29.280 --> 00:03:30.540
We had to finish the show.

00:03:30.540 --> 00:03:33.000
That was like a part of my family.

00:03:33.000 --> 00:03:34.380
Linda Cardellini and Liz Feldman,

00:03:34.380 --> 00:03:35.520
everyone there is my family

00:03:35.520 --> 00:03:37.770
and there's a story that
we needed to finish.

00:03:37.770 --> 00:03:40.020
And I pushed as hard as
I could through that.

00:03:40.020 --> 00:03:42.210
So, it was really incredibly difficult.

00:03:42.210 --> 00:03:44.760
And then I went to sleep for a few months

00:03:44.760 --> 00:03:47.400
and then all of a sudden
now I had to come out again

00:03:47.400 --> 00:03:49.350
and be this person that-

00:03:49.350 --> 00:03:50.970
People had seen me as this other person

00:03:50.970 --> 00:03:53.267
for the last almost 40 years

00:03:53.267 --> 00:03:56.733
and I'm different now
and it's incredibly hard.

00:03:58.398 --> 00:04:01.320
I don't wanna be the
fish in the fish tank,

00:04:01.320 --> 00:04:06.320
but I'm gonna do my best to
just get through it I suppose.

00:04:06.597 --> 00:04:09.330
I think that's the answer
I'm trying to get to, but-

00:04:09.330 --> 00:04:10.980
- Yeah, but do you feel like-

00:04:10.980 --> 00:04:13.348
I feel like sometimes,

00:04:13.348 --> 00:04:15.960
well and this is a
completely separate thing

00:04:15.960 --> 00:04:17.130
obviously not comparing,

00:04:17.130 --> 00:04:19.290
but just going through any kind of grief

00:04:19.290 --> 00:04:22.358
like I've gone through grief
and it's like, sometimes

00:04:22.358 --> 00:04:26.220
for me it's nice to have
something to be productive.

00:04:26.220 --> 00:04:27.450
There's something to do

00:04:27.450 --> 00:04:30.390
so I'm not just sitting in my thoughts.

00:04:30.390 --> 00:04:33.693
Are you like that? Do you
like to be a little busy?

00:04:34.949 --> 00:04:37.680
- I've probably been going through grief

00:04:37.680 --> 00:04:38.940
and trauma my whole life

00:04:38.940 --> 00:04:42.240
and acting was the place that I got to go

00:04:42.240 --> 00:04:45.973
to not feel it, you know?

00:04:45.973 --> 00:04:47.072
- Oh wow.

00:04:47.072 --> 00:04:50.910
- So, I think that I went
through many breakups

00:04:50.910 --> 00:04:53.280
during television shows,
I went through trauma,

00:04:53.280 --> 00:04:56.220
I went through deaths
I went through cancer,

00:04:56.220 --> 00:04:57.090
I went through all this stuff

00:04:57.090 --> 00:05:02.090
and I always had a place to go
to kind of not deal with it.

00:05:02.490 --> 00:05:04.018
And the beauty of Dead to Me

00:05:04.018 --> 00:05:07.830
is that it gave me almost
this weird platform

00:05:07.830 --> 00:05:09.690
of dealing with it.

00:05:09.690 --> 00:05:12.960
Where I didn't have to be on all the time

00:05:12.960 --> 00:05:15.120
and I didn't have to make all the jokes

00:05:15.120 --> 00:05:16.590
and I could fall apart in a scene.

00:05:16.590 --> 00:05:20.550
And it was like me. It was my
soul actually falling apart.

00:05:20.550 --> 00:05:23.700
And unfortunately in front of the world.

00:05:23.700 --> 00:05:27.618
But it was cathartic in
a in a beautiful way.

00:05:27.618 --> 00:05:30.098
So, yeah, I hear you honey.

00:05:30.098 --> 00:05:31.710
We're work horses.

00:05:31.710 --> 00:05:33.420
You've been doing it since you were young

00:05:33.420 --> 00:05:36.060
and it's like the place we go

00:05:36.060 --> 00:05:38.700
because we don't know how else
to deal with all the noise.

00:05:38.700 --> 00:05:40.380
But, yeah.

00:05:40.380 --> 00:05:41.850
- No, I get that a little too

00:05:41.850 --> 00:05:43.320
because I'm like that on stage

00:05:43.320 --> 00:05:44.250
- I think you and I are
going to be best friends

00:05:44.250 --> 00:05:45.120
pretty much after this

00:05:45.120 --> 00:05:46.800
- I think we're very similar.

00:05:46.800 --> 00:05:48.120
I've seen you in interviews.

00:05:48.120 --> 00:05:50.532
I Google everyone and I go
down the path on YouTube

00:05:50.532 --> 00:05:52.200
whenever I'm interviewing people.

00:05:52.200 --> 00:05:55.290
So I do think we would
get along really well.

00:05:55.290 --> 00:05:58.053
I mean, how difficult was
it to keep working though?

00:05:59.390 --> 00:06:04.230
'Cause I have people in my
life that I know that have MS

00:06:04.230 --> 00:06:05.850
but I've never experienced it personally

00:06:05.850 --> 00:06:07.830
so I never like to pretend
I know what that's like.

00:06:07.830 --> 00:06:11.160
So it's like, it has to be
wearing on you emotionally,

00:06:11.160 --> 00:06:12.510
mentally, and physically.

00:06:12.510 --> 00:06:14.280
How was that to keep working?

00:06:14.280 --> 00:06:16.200
- Well, there's four
different kinds of MS.

00:06:16.200 --> 00:06:19.320
So not everyone is the same.

00:06:19.320 --> 00:06:20.730
Everyone's symptoms are different.

00:06:20.730 --> 00:06:22.890
Everyone's experience
with it is different.

00:06:22.890 --> 00:06:24.918
Every day is different.

00:06:24.918 --> 00:06:27.600
So that is just so that
people understand that

00:06:27.600 --> 00:06:29.670
'cause people are like, well,
why don't you like this one

00:06:29.670 --> 00:06:30.870
and you're feeling better like this one.

00:06:30.870 --> 00:06:34.110
And it's like everyone has a
different experience with it.

00:06:34.110 --> 00:06:36.480
So that's just a preface.

00:06:36.480 --> 00:06:38.130
Shooting the show was the hardest thing

00:06:38.130 --> 00:06:39.690
I'd ever done in my life

00:06:39.690 --> 00:06:42.900
because I was diagnosed during shooting

00:06:42.900 --> 00:06:44.940
and I didn't know what
was happening to me.

00:06:44.940 --> 00:06:46.890
I couldn't walk,

00:06:46.890 --> 00:06:50.160
they had to use a
wheelchair to get me to set.

00:06:50.160 --> 00:06:52.980
I was freaking out until someone
was like, you need an MRI.

00:06:52.980 --> 00:06:55.710
And then I found out
on a Monday after work

00:06:55.710 --> 00:06:56.643
that I had MS.

00:06:57.750 --> 00:07:00.300
A disease that I'm gonna
have for the rest of my life.

00:07:00.300 --> 00:07:03.240
And then I started thinking
about the last four years

00:07:03.240 --> 00:07:06.330
and I had very small symptoms.

00:07:06.330 --> 00:07:08.400
And I think that's important for people.

00:07:08.400 --> 00:07:09.930
Let's not all be hypochondriacs

00:07:09.930 --> 00:07:12.561
and run to the doctor
when our leg gets weak.

00:07:12.561 --> 00:07:14.280
But-
- But be aware.

00:07:14.280 --> 00:07:15.840
- That was happening to me four years ago

00:07:15.840 --> 00:07:17.707
where we were on set and I'd go like,

00:07:17.707 --> 00:07:20.433
"Ooh, I think I'm tired", you know?

00:07:21.390 --> 00:07:24.600
So it presented itself
like a few years ago

00:07:24.600 --> 00:07:27.870
until it just got as bad as it did.

00:07:27.870 --> 00:07:30.750
- That's hard, Christina.
That's hard.

00:07:30.750 --> 00:07:32.370
Getting that call and being at work

00:07:32.370 --> 00:07:35.670
and having that kind of news laid on you

00:07:35.670 --> 00:07:38.650
and ooh, that's really hard.

00:07:38.650 --> 00:07:41.258
- Can I say it sucked balls?

00:07:41.258 --> 00:07:42.091
(crowd laughing)

00:07:42.091 --> 00:07:44.463
- Yeah, you can say
whatever the hell you want.

